<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sandilch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sandilch was a ruler of the Utigurs, a nomadic people of the steppe east of the Don, in the mid-6th century. Known from the Byzantine historians <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a>, he was persuaded by Emperor Justinian, with gifts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, to attack the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> in 551 and again after their raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 559. The resulting wars exhausted both peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandilch.jpg" alt="Sandilch" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Homann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ukrania_quae_et_Terra_Cosaccorum_cum_vicinis_Walachiae,_Moldoviae,_Johann_Baptiste_Homann_(Nuremberg,_1720).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
